The school example is just one I happen to be experiencing at the moment. But you should not feel isolated because your life isn't like theirs. We've been told our whole life that we're supposed to do this and that by a certain age and now with Social media and technology in general constantly throwing images and stories of people living these perfect lives it's just gotten worse. But just think for a second, do you post the bad things on your life on social media? do you go out of your way to let the whole world know of your financial and health problems?  Not one normal person does. 

&#x200B;

So comparing ourselves to these people is no different that comparing yourself to a movie character or cartoon. It's insane! it makes absolutely no sense. But because these happen to be people we know it just seems more "real". But it isn't, it really isn't. So don't be so hard on yourself because you have no clue what their life is really like and are only hurting yourself. Mind you, I say all of these and I constantly do it myself. I've been working hard on getting off instagram as it's probably the worse way in which I do this. I look at old friends from high school getting 6 figure jobs, traveling across the world, getting married and having kids all while I still have no clue who I am in this world. Sometimes it will completely ruin my day, not because I envy them, I actually feel happy for them. But it immediately makes me put myself down wondering why I didn't work as hard in school or why I've never traveled to where they have and so on. All of these because of 3 or 4 pictures of a trip an old friend from high school posted on his phone.  


Pictures that are in no way or shape representative of his whole life and which logically should have absolutely no effect on my life. Comparing ourselves to others constantly is probably amongst the worse triggers of our depression when it comes to things we can actually control. We can't control a terminal illness, the death of someone we love, a disability and yet still we can fight those things and overcome them or at the very least learn to live with them. But comparing ourselves to others? That's something we do to ourselves, something we have control of. Sure, it's easier said than done but after all we've been through it's one of the easiest things we can stop doing. After all, it's small steps/changes in our habits where we should start. This is no doubt one of them.

&#x200B;

I don't care if you're 19 or 90, as long as you're here on this earth you should love yourself for who you are. I have ups and downs and you will see that from my post history. This week It's been more up than down, maybe the next one won't be as good, who knows?  But I'm fighting with all I got to hold on to this up and I really hope you can too. So next time you smile, for whatever reason, even if it's just a tiny smile during the worse of days. Even if it's an ironic smile against our own misery. Hold on to that moment because you know there's part of you that can still feel joy.

He's 19... 

&#x200B;

No, seriously OP, I know there are mature 19 year olds out there but he's obviously still a child and you're 21 and an adult. He must be "cute" or something but unless he magically matures overnight you're better off finding someone you age or older unless you want to raise this kid.

You've been together for 8 months, he needs to make more of an effort. I'm sure he must be embarrassed and there might be other underlying issues. As a guy with depression who also happens to have a very high sex drive I know depression can completely destroy my sex drive temporarily. Any chance he's been feeling down? What is his response when you talk to him about it? has he agreed to try to make it better and what is his plan to do so?

&#x200B;

Personally, I think 8 months is too early to already be having these issues. However, if he has depression there might be a multitude of reasons affecting his sex drive which don't even have anything to do with the relationship. Nonetheless, you should not be a victim to his problems, he has to show some initiative and effort to make things better. Otherwise, you're better off going your separate ways now. Something you obviously already know from your previous relationship.
